WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
He was

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.280
sharing photos

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.400
of a woman who was in toilet. If that is

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.120
not bizarre enough, I don't know what it

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.000
is. Right now, even for Europe, it's a

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.760
big challenge because it is again and

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.240
again things like chat controller are

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.880
coming back with a different name.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.320
>> Is your vacuum cleaner spying on you?

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.600
Sounds like a joke, but it's not.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.160
In this episode, I had the chance to

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.800
talk to Kushal Das. Kushal is a public

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.280
interest technologist and core team

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.200
member at the Tor Project, core

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.480
developer of CPython, and director of

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.680
the Python Software Foundation.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.880
We talked about privacy, surveillance,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.680
and what it actually takes to protect

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.040
people online.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.400
On your t-shirt, it's written that

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.880
privacy is a human right. Is it?

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.840
>> Yes, we believe so, and it's extremely

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.920
important that many people actually get

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.200
reminded again and again because in the

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.360
modern world and thanks to this thing

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.520
called computer and all the new

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.600
technologies we're building around, it's

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.080
like it's

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.320
keeping it as a saying human right is

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.520
becoming a very difficult. And many of

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.360
the parts of the world we actually do

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.800
not have that as a right.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.040
I I believe so firmly.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.640
>> And what sparked your passion for

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.240
privacy? In the past, you were focused

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.200
on technologies, but nowadays you're

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.480
focusing on freedom and privacy. How did

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.360
it all started?

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.400
>> So, as you mentioned, yes, I was more

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.200
interested about technology in general,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.000
like learning things, how to build or

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.960
how to break. All of them were fun, but

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.440
at the very I would say the beginning of

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.080
my technology journey,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.120
I was lucky enough to meet few or made

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.920
few friends from Chaos Computer Club

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.320
Berlin. And a lot of ideas have about

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.760
hacking, how technology works, how to

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.640
make things better for the world comes

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.960
from there. But also along with the

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.920
started, you know, you started growing

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.120
mentally a little bit, read more, learn

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.520
more what happened before in the world.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.400
And I was lucky enough to get married to

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.840
a lawyer, Anwesha, my wife. I mean,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.160
she's lawyer by degree. So, after the

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.280
marriage, started learning a lot more,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.640
things I had no clue about how world

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.360
works. I almost spoke Bengali, but yeah.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.040
And that's when like more and more

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.640
slowly I was like like I think it

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.080
started at home. And then like I was

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.360
getting more and more focused on like at

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.800
first maybe in the security side, but

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.280
then it like oh

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.760
like we need more people looking at

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.760
privacy and rights and like freedom.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.320
It's very easy to say that we are free,

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.000
but if we start like are we? When people

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.080
ask, then start looking at things. So,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.120
that's how I slowly moved more and more

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.920
like oh, we need more people who can

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.400
understand or do things. So, that's how

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.240
jumped from technology to privacy and

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.400
security like things. And focusing more

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.760
on privacy, helping people out.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.080
And I believe I think as you said like

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.600
in the beginning like

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.440
things are becoming very complex. So,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.400
having that rights or understanding is

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.080
very difficult right now.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.600
>> One of the projects you were working on

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.880
and maintaining for a long time is

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.680
SecureDrop. Can you tell us something a

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.440
bit more about this project?

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.440
>> Yes. So, SecureDrop it started long time

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.080
ago as a different name by

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.480
Aaron Swartz, who is sadly no longer

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.400
with us anymore. But after he passed

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.480
away, it

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.120
it was I think that then it was named

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.959
was DeadDrop, if I remember correctly.

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:21.440
But then Freedom of the Press Foundation

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.400
took over the project and it became

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.800
SecureDrop, became much bigger. It's an

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.560
open slow open-source whistleblower

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.519
platform. Which is used across the world

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:33.320
where people can, you know, leak

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.840
information without the fear of any

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.920
three-letter agency. It's written in

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.080
Python. It uses the same technology we

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.480
use like day in our daily life, same

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.519
tools which we used to build our other

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:47.840
corporate projects. And it also uses the

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.360
privacy aspect which comes from the Tor

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.920
project itself. And along with all the

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.960
different things and more

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.040
particular focus on security, it helps

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.800
people to, you know, become a

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.600
whistleblower and leak and talk to the

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.160
journalist in a better way.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.080
>> So, how does it work? If I were a

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.480
journalist, for example, or a

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.800
whistleblower in a country with a very

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.080
oppressive regime, how could I talk to

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.480
someone by using SecureDrop?

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.000
>> So, generally the organizations, there

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.120
are [snorts] multiple place, one is

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.320
SecureDrop. They have like there is also

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.079
in the website there is a directory.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:23.400
Many news orgs, when they run in their

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.000
main newspaper, if they have physical

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.919
newspaper or in their website, they

00:04:26.919 --> 00:04:29.040
provide the URL or they give a page

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.360
where they talk about SecureDrop. Like

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.360
the idea is generally you do use

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.000
something called Tails, if possible.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.720
If nothing else and or a normal uh

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.400
Tor browser, connect open that dot onion

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.880
site, which is the

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.160
like Tor service.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.080
And after you open that onion address,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.960
you you can actually create your first

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.320
time. When you create your account first

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.320
time or when you say that I want to log

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.000
in first time, you get a one-time

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.160
password, which is also both your

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.280
username and password at the same time.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.240
And that's what I I will remember. I did

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.960
not look into the new like UI, if there

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.600
is any more changes. And then you can

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.640
what you can do is you can upload

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.160
documents your you can write down text

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.080
and journalists

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.440
they have a special Qubes OS based uh

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.200
which is a Linux based operating system

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.000
where every app can run a separate VM

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.520
and they can connect to via Tor to their

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.440
server in a different interface and they

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:29.360
can decrypt it in a safe environment and

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:31.480
see the messages.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:32.240
>> And what were the [clears throat]

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.480
biggest technical challenges when you

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.160
were implementing it?

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.960
>> I'll say like keep

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.600
one thing in general, not only for

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.200
SecureDrop or any project which talks

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.360
about security and privacy and then the

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.800
word other term is usability. I think

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.400
that's always a big challenge. We can

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.640
make things really secure. I guess like

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.320
the always we always joke about it that

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.000
computer which never boots the thing

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.120
that's the best secure computer. So

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.680
that's always a challenge like how can

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.360
you keep security and privacy of people

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.040
and data while

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.440
it's me keeping it usable.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.720
>> So I think at the big like long time ago

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.480
it was I discovered that the project

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.280
that like the journalist what they see

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.320
and how they do, they can do it far

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.680
better if they have only like in from

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.600
Qubes OS in instead of going into a

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.720
secure room using on one particular

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.680
computer with Tails.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.800
But maybe using Qubes is a easier

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.520
solution for them. So I mean it enables

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:31.000
us the community enables us to do this

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.720
>> And how did you earn trust for people

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.280
living in those regimes because I guess

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:37.960
that for those people they were

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:43.040
basically risking their life by

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.360
Um how did you earn their trust? Have

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.960
you ever had any issues with people for

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.520
example not trusting you or being afraid

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.440
to use it?

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.640
>> So here is a good thing about SecureDrop

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:56.200
or the project itself. The project

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.040
people don't have anything to do with

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.800
the actual whistleblowing itself. They

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.400
when people whistleblow, they

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.600
whistleblow a particular news

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.480
